Article 10 (Decisions on Permission to Conduct Litigation)
(1) A written decision granting or refusing permission to conduct litigation shall state the following matters and shall bear the name and seal of the judge who made the decision:
1. The plaintiff and the plaintiff's counsel;
2. The defendant;
3. The operative part of the decision;
4. The reasons for the decision.
(2) The reasons stated in a written decision refusing permission to conduct litigation shall specify the requirements for permission that have not been satisfied.
(3) Decisions granting or refusing permission to conduct litigation shall be served on the plaintiff and defendant by sending certified copies of the decisions.
(4) Where a decision refusing permission to conduct litigation becomes final and conclusive, the collective litigation shall be deemed not to have been filed.
